{"qid":"Q1032","names":["Republic of the Niger"],"title":"Republic of the Niger","endonym":{"native":"Nijar","lang":"ha"},"capsule":"The Republic of Niger is a landlocked Sahelian state in West Africa, achieving independence from France on 3 August 1960 after a decade as an autonomous territory within the French Community. Its political history has been marked by repeated military coups — in 1974, 1996, 1999, and 2010 — punctuating civilian governments; uranium deposits discovered in the Agadez region made Niger a significant exporter from the 1970s but did not translate into broad development. A prolonged Tuareg rebellion in the north (1990–1995 and 2007–2009) tested the state's authority over its vast desert territory. In July 2023 the Presidential Guard overthrew President Mohamed Bazoum, placing Niger under a military junta that suspended constitutional institutions.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Niger's state boundaries are those established at independence from French administrative demarcation and are internationally recognized; the northern desert boundary with Algeria and Libya follows colonial-era lines whose precision in remote areas is limited.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Niger","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Niger"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Niger","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Niger"}],"britannica":"place/Niger","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Niger","rulers":[{"qid":"Q314355","name":"Diori Hamani","start":1960,"end":1974,"image":"Hamani Diori 1968b.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hamani%20Diori%201968b.jpg?width=320","imag